// Source locations.
//
// `Location` is what `#caller_location` evaluates to (§5.2). It is an ordinary
// struct — the compiler finds it by its `#lang` tag, never by name or path, so a
// `core` that spells it differently still works.
//
// The only way to get one is as a **default argument**:
//
//     report :: func (msg: str, loc: Location := #caller_location) { ... }
//
// which is the whole point. A default is filled in at the call site, so each
// call supplies *its own* position — that is what makes `panic` name the line
// that called it rather than the line inside `core` that raised it. Written
// anywhere else, `#caller_location` would have to mean "the location of this
// expression", which is a different and much less useful thing, so it is
// refused.
//
// The fields are `@public(all)`: a location that cannot be read is not a
// location.

@public(all)
Location :: #lang("location") struct {
  // The source file, as the compiler was given it.
  file: str,
  // 1-based, counted the way an editor counts.
  line:   u32,
  // 1-based, counted in `char`s from the start of the line — not in bytes, so a
  // line with text outside ASCII still points where a reader would look.
  column: u32,
}
